Q: Query with SQL-like joins between XML files A: <p>xmlstarlet uses XPath 1.0, which is not relationally complete - meaning it cannot do general joins in the SQL sense. Ideally you want to use XQuery, which is designed for this job.</p>
<p>You can then do queries like</p>
<pre><code>for $person in doc('persons.xml')//person
for $location in doc('locations.xml')//town
where $person/town = $location/name
return
  &lt;person&gt;
    {$person/*, $location/country}
  &lt;/person&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Java SQL cant insert Date and Time A: <p>Your code has multiple significant problems.</p>
<h2>Issues</h2>
<h3><em>java.time</em></h3>
<p>Firstly, never use the terribly-flawed legacy classes such as <code>Date</code> &amp; <code>Time</code>.</p>
<p>Use only the modern <em>java.time</em> classes in Java 8+. Support for <em>java.time</em> is required in all <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JDBC_driver" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JDBC 4.2+ drivers</a>.</p>
<h3>Security</h3>
<p>Secondly, your code has security flaws, risking <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL_injection" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SQL Injection</a> attacks.</p>
<p>Write your SQL with question-mark characters rather than user-input strings: <code>VALUES ( ? , ? , ? )</code>.</p>
<h3>Close resources</h3>
<p>Also, you should use <a href="https://www.baeldung.com/java-try-with-resources" rel="nofollow noreferrer">try-with-resources</a> syntax to automatically close your database resources.</p>
<h2>UI code</h2>
<p>Your user-interface code should be something like this:</p>
