</code></pre>
<p>… and:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String eventInput = IO.readln( &quot;Enter Event: &quot; ) ;
if ( eventInput.isBlank() ) { … }
</code></pre>
<h2>SQL code</h2>
<p>Your database code should be something like the following.</p>
<p>Use <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/378" rel="nofollow noreferrer">text blocks</a> for easier editing of your SQL.</p>
<p>Your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integrated_development_environment" rel="nofollow noreferrer">IDE</a> may be able to recognize your text block contains SQL if you provide a hint in a comment such as <code>// language=SQL</code>. Some IDEs may read a comment above the text block. For <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IntelliJ_IDEA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">IntelliJ</a> specifically, add the <a href="https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/annotating-source-code.html#jetbrains-annotations" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>JetBrains Annotations</em> dependency</a> to your project for its <code>@Language</code> annotation.</p>
<p>When naming SQL objects (tables, columns, etc.), do not use CamelCase. Use either all UPPERCASE or all lowercase. Technically, the SQL standard requires a database engine to keep names in all UPPERCASE, but this rule is ignored by many database products. So in practice, it is usually best to use all lowercase, with underscore character as delimiter.</p>
<p>As a further tip, I recommend ending all database names with an underscore character to avoid any naming collision with keywords. The SQL standard explicitly promises to never use a trailing underscore in its keywords.</p>
